cli.error.version_args [error]: {version} takes no arguments, not "{argument}". See: {argument}: string, the first word after version, as typed; {version}: string, the version subcommand (fixed), version
link.error.backup_exists [error]: Cannot move {target} aside: {backup} is already there. Try again in a second. {target}: path, what is in the way, ~-shortened; {backup}: path, the backup name it would get, ~-shortened
link.status.interrupted [status]: Stopped; nothing else was moved or linked. Run {self}{link} again to finish. {self}: string, the command word: the name when one works on this machine, else homebot (filled by the renderer); {link}: string, the link subcommand suffix (fixed),  link
layout.inside_repo [error]: would be linked at {target}, inside the home repo; rename it {target}: path, where in $HOME the name maps to, ~-shortened
layout.dot_parens [error]: "{raw}" names no folder: {dot_names} would link outside the folder it is in; rename it {raw}: string, the name as written, (.) or (..); {dot_names}: string, the two dot folder names (fixed), . and ..
layout.outside_folder [error]: would be linked at {target}, outside the folder it is in; rename it {target}: path, where in $HOME (or above it) the name maps to, ~-shortened
keys.prompt.passphrase [prompt]: Enter passphrase (leave empty to autogenerate a secure one):
keys.prompt.confirm [prompt]: Confirm passphrase:
keys.status.autogenerated [status]: using autogenerated passphrase "{passphrase}" {passphrase}: string, the passphrase homebot made up: ten words joined by hyphens
keys.error.mismatch [error]: The passphrases didn't match; nothing was saved.
unlock.prompt.passphrase [prompt]: Enter passphrase:
secrets.error.no_terminal [error]: There is no terminal to ask for the passphrase on. Run this in a terminal.
secrets.error.cancelled [error]: Stopped at the passphrase prompt; nothing changed.
secrets.error.identity_unreadable [error]: {identity} is not a passphrase-encrypted age key. Restore it from the repo's history. {identity}: string, the encrypted identity path (fixed), private/identity.age
secrets.error.empty_recipients [error]: {recipients} has no public key in it. Restore it from the repo's history. {recipients}: string, the recipients path (fixed), private/recipients.txt
secrets.error.reserved [error]: Refusing to write over {path}: it is not a secret. {path}: path, the file relative to the repo, such as private/identity.age
secrets.error.unsupported_ssh_key [error]: Line {line} of {recipients} holds a key age cannot encrypt to ({key_type}). Use an {ed25519} key, or an {rsa} key of at least {bits}; homebot never seals to fewer keys than the file lists. {line}: int, the line's number in private/recipients.txt, counting from 1; {key_type}: string, the key's type as the line names it, such as ecdsa-sha2-nistp256, sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com, ssh-dss, or ssh-rsa for a key under 2048 bits; {recipients}: string, the recipients path (fixed), private/recipients.txt; {ed25519}: string, the supported SSH key type (fixed), ssh-ed25519; {rsa}: string, the supported SSH key type (fixed), ssh-rsa; {bits}: string, the minimum RSA size (fixed), 2048 bits
secrets.error.save_key [error]: Saving the new key to {path} failed; nothing was saved. {path}: path, private/identity.age or private/recipients.txt, relative to the repo
secrets.error.save_gitignore [error]: Writing {path} failed, so nothing was set up: without it, git would take the plaintext secrets in {private}. {path}: path, private/.gitignore, relative to the repo; {private}: string, the private directory (fixed), private/
pack.error.private_reserved [error]: {path} can't go into {private}: git would stage it as plaintext there. {readme}, {gitignore}, {gitkeep} and {recipients} files, {age_glob} files and {git_dir} folders are the repo's own; rename or remove it first. {path}: path, the first thing inside the packed path (or the path itself) with such a name, ~-shortened; {private}: string, the private secrets directory (fixed), private/; {readme}: string, the reserved readme file (fixed), README.md; {gitignore}: string, the reserved ignore file (fixed), .gitignore; {gitkeep}: string, the reserved placeholder file (fixed), .gitkeep; {recipients}: string, the recipients file (fixed), recipients.txt; {age_glob}: string, the encrypted file pattern (fixed), *.age; {git_dir}: string, the git metadata directory (fixed), .git
pack.error.nested_repo [error]: {path} is a git repository of its own: the home repo would hold only a pointer to it, and its files would never reach the remote. Remove its {git_dir}, or leave it out of the home. {path}: path, the folder holding the nested .git, ~-shortened: the packed path or one inside it, or the repo path triage tried to stage; {git_dir}: string, the git metadata directory (fixed), .git
pack.error.unreadable [error]: Can't read {path}, so homebot can't check it for a git repository of its own. Nothing was moved; make it readable, or leave it out of the home. {path}: path, the folder inside the packed path that could not be read, ~-shortened
pack.error.layer_not_linked [error]: The {layer} layer is not linked on {os}, so a file packed there could not be linked back (use {layers}). {layer}: string, the --layer value; {os}: string, this machine's system (linux or macos); {layers}: string, the layers linked on this machine, comma-joined
pack.error.source_not_removed [error]: Copied {path} into the repo as {repo_path}, but could not remove the original: {reason}
Remove it, then link the copy with: {path}: path, the packed path, ~-shortened; {repo_path}: path, where the copy is, relative to the repo root; {reason}: string, what the system said
pack.status.moved [status]: moved {from} → {to} {from}: path, the packed path, ~-shortened; {to}: path, where it is now, relative to the repo root
setup.error.git_too_old [error]: Your git is {found}, and homebot needs {minimum} or newer: its commit guard relies on a git command that older versions lack, so every save would be refused. Update git, then run {command} again. {found}: string, the version git reports, such as 2.39.5; {minimum}: string, the oldest git homebot accepts, 2.40; {command}: string, the setup command (fixed), {self} setup
setup.error.git_too_old_brew [error]: Your git is {found}, and homebot needs {minimum} or newer: its commit guard relies on a git command that older versions lack, so every save would be refused. Install a newer one with Homebrew, then run {setup} again:
  {command} {found}: string, the version git reports, such as 2.39.5; {minimum}: string, the oldest git homebot accepts, 2.40; {setup}: string, the command to run again (fixed), {self} setup; {command}: string, the command that installs a newer git (fixed), brew install git
setup.error.xdg_relative [error]: {name} is "{value}", which is not a full path, so the background helper can't use it. Set it to a full path, or unset it, then run {command} again. {name}: string, XDG_CONFIG_HOME, XDG_DATA_HOME, XDG_STATE_HOME or XDG_CACHE_HOME; {value}: string, its value as set; {command}: string, the setup command (fixed), {self} setup
setup.hint.xdg [hint]: The helper keeps the {xdg_glob} folders it was set up with. After changing {config_env}, {data_env}, {state_env} or {cache_env}, run {self}{setup} again. {self}: string, the command word: the name when one works on this machine, else homebot (filled by the renderer); {xdg_glob}: string, the XDG variable family (fixed), XDG_*; {config_env}: string, the config environment variable (fixed), XDG_CONFIG_HOME; {data_env}: string, the data environment variable (fixed), XDG_DATA_HOME; {state_env}: string, the state environment variable (fixed), XDG_STATE_HOME; {cache_env}: string, the cache environment variable (fixed), XDG_CACHE_HOME; {setup}: string, the setup subcommand suffix (fixed),  setup
setup.hint.keys [hint]: Secrets aren't set up yet. To keep passwords and tokens in the repo, encrypted, create the keys:
setup.prompt.install_tools [prompt]: Missing: {tools}. Install now with {command}? {yn} {tools}: string, the missing tools, comma-separated, such as gitleaks; {command}: string, the install command, such as brew install gitleaks; {yn}: string, the answers, Enter meaning yes (fixed), [Y/n]
setup.error.tools_missing [error]: Missing: {tools}. The commit guard needs it to check every commit. Install it, then run {setup} again:
  {command} {tools}: string, the missing tools, comma-separated, such as gitleaks; {setup}: string, the command to run again (fixed), {self} setup; {command}: string, the install command, such as brew install gitleaks
setup.error.install_failed [error]: {command} failed. Install {tools} yourself, then run {setup} again. {command}: string, the install command that failed, such as brew install gitleaks; {tools}: string, the missing tools, comma-separated; {setup}: string, the command to run again (fixed), {self} setup
setup.error.home_taken [error]: {path} is there but holds no {config}, so it isn't a home repo and setup won't touch it. Move it aside, or set {env} to another folder, then run {setup} again:
  {command} {path}: path, where setup would put the home repo: ~/.home, or $HOMEBOT_ROOT; {config}: string, the file that marks a home repo (fixed), homebot.yml; {env}: string, the variable that points homebot at its repo (fixed), HOMEBOT_ROOT; {setup}: string, the command to run again (fixed), {self} setup; {command}: string, the command that moves it aside: mv ~/.home ~/.home.old
setup.error.no_terminal [error]: No home repo yet, and setup starts or clones one only in a terminal. Run it in one:
  {setup}
Or clone yours by hand:
  {clone} {path} {setup}: string, the command to run in a terminal (fixed), {self} setup; {clone}: string, the clone command, the URL left to fill in (fixed), git clone <url>; {path}: path, where the home repo goes: ~/.home, or $HOMEBOT_ROOT
setup.status.no_repo [status]: No home repo yet.
setup.prompt.url [prompt]: URL of your home repo, or {example} on GitHub: {example}: string, the short form setup takes for a GitHub repository (fixed), owner/repo
setup.status.cloning [status]: Cloning {url} into {path}... {url}: string, the address git clones from; {path}: path, where the home repo goes: ~/.home, or $HOMEBOT_ROOT
setup.warning.clone_failed [warning]: Couldn't clone {url}. {url}: string, the address git could not clone
setup.error.clone_failed [error]: Couldn't clone {url}. Check the address and that you can reach it, then run {setup} again. {url}: string, the address git could not clone; {setup}: string, the command to run again (fixed), {self} setup
setup.error.clone_auth_gh [error]: git couldn't sign in to GitHub to clone {url}. Sign in with the GitHub CLI, which sets git up to use that login, then run {setup} again:
  {command} {url}: string, the address git could not clone; {setup}: string, the command to run again (fixed), {self} setup; {command}: string, the command that signs in to GitHub and sets git up for it (fixed), gh auth login
setup.error.clone_auth_key [error]: git couldn't sign in to {host} to clone {url}. Add an ssh key to your account there, then run {setup} again. {url}: string, the address git could not clone; {host}: string, the server of that address, such as github.com; {setup}: string, the command to run again (fixed), {self} setup
setup.error.root_relative [error]: {env} is "{value}", which is not a full path, so homebot would find the repo only from this folder. Set it to a full path, or unset it, then run {setup} again. {env}: string, the variable that points homebot at its repo (fixed), HOMEBOT_ROOT; {value}: string, its value as set; {setup}: string, the command to run again (fixed), {self} setup
setup.error.not_a_home_repo [error]: {url} has no {config}, so it isn't a home repo. It is in {path} now: move it aside, then run {setup} again. {url}: string, the address setup cloned; {config}: string, the file that marks a home repo (fixed), homebot.yml; {path}: path, where it was cloned: ~/.home, or $HOMEBOT_ROOT; {setup}: string, the command to run again (fixed), {self} setup
setup.success.created [success]: created {path}, a new home repo with a {profile} profile {path}: path, the new home repo: ~/.home, or $HOMEBOT_ROOT; {profile}: string, the profile a new repo starts with (fixed), base
setup.prompt.keys [prompt]: Keep passwords and tokens in the repo, encrypted? This creates your keys. {yn} {yn}: string, the answers, Enter meaning yes (fixed), [Y/n]
setup.warning.no_profile [warning]: No profile for this machine ({host}) yet, so nothing was unpacked. Name one in {config} under {hosts} or {default}, then: {host}: string, this machine's short host name; {config}: string, the file that names the profiles (fixed), homebot.yml; {hosts}: string, the key that gives a profile per host (fixed), profiles.hosts; {default}: string, the key that gives the profile of every other host (fixed), profiles.default
setup.prompt.unpack [prompt]: Run {n:this step|these # steps}? {yn} {n}: int, how many steps the profile has; {yn}: string, the answers, Enter meaning yes (fixed), [Y/n]
setup.prompt.backup [prompt]: {n:# file is|# files are} in the way of links. Move them aside (nothing is deleted) and link? {yn} {n}: int, how many links are blocked by real files; {yn}: string, the answers, Enter meaning yes (fixed), [Y/n]
setup.prompt.zshrc [prompt]: Add it to {file} now? {yn} {file}: path, the .zshrc setup appends to: ~/.zshrc, or $ZDOTDIR/.zshrc; {yn}: string, the answers, Enter meaning yes (fixed), [Y/n]
setup.success.zshrc [success]: added the startup line to {file} {file}: path, the .zshrc setup appended to
setup.status.zshrc_in_repo [status]: That file links into your home repo, so {path} changed there. {path}: string, the repo file the .zshrc links to, relative to the repo
setup.status.zshrc_linked [status]: {file} is a link to {target}, and setup writes only through a link to a file in your home repo. Add the line there yourself. {file}: path, the .zshrc setup would append to: ~/.zshrc, or $ZDOTDIR/.zshrc; {target}: path, where the link points: its resolved file, or for a dangling link the path it names
setup.status.zsh_only [status]: Your shell is {shell}. The startup line and the prompt count are {zsh}-only for now. {shell}: string, the login shell's name, from $SHELL, such as bash; {zsh}: string, the one shell the integration supports (fixed), zsh
setup.prompt.triage [prompt]: {n:# thing|# things} in your home could be tracked. Sort them now, one key each? {yn} {n}: int, how many things triage would ask about; {yn}: string, the answers, Enter meaning yes (fixed), [Y/n]
setup.prompt.save [prompt]: Commit and push now? {yn} {yn}: string, the answers, Enter meaning yes (fixed), [Y/n]
setup.prompt.save_local [prompt]: Commit now? {yn} {yn}: string, the answers, Enter meaning yes (fixed), [Y/n]
tidy.error.lock [error]: Could not lock {path}: {reason}. tidy takes a file lock so two runs never overlap, and the filesystem holding the state directory does not support it. Point {env} at a local disk. {path}: string, the tidy.lock file; {reason}: string, what the system said, such as function not implemented; {env}: string, the state environment variable (fixed), XDG_STATE_HOME
tidy.error.autosave_no_guard [error]: skipped, because the commit guard could not be installed this run (see the guard step)
inbox.helper_no_linger.title [inbox]: The background helper stops when you log out
inbox.helper_no_linger.detail [inbox]: systemd ends your timers with your last session; with lingering on it keeps them running
config.error.persona [error]: {where}: {key} must be a string: {allowed} {where}: string, where in the file: '<file>:<line>:<col>'; {key}: string, the persona config key (fixed), persona; {allowed}: string, the allowed persona values (fixed), none or a lowercase id of at most 32 letters, digits and hyphens, starting with a letter
config.error.persona_syntax [error]: {where}: {key} "{value}" must be {allowed} {where}: string, where in the file: '<file>:<line>:<col>'; {value}: string, the invalid persona id with unsafe control characters removed; {key}: string, the persona config key (fixed), persona; {allowed}: string, the allowed persona values (fixed), none or a lowercase id of at most 32 letters, digits and hyphens, starting with a letter
persona.success.selected [success]: {id} is selected. {id}: string, selected persona id
persona.success.greeting [success]: {bot} is ready. {bot}: string, the speaker name
persona.success.created [success]: Created persona file: {path} {path}: path, new persona file
config.error.name [error]: {where}: {name_key} must be a word, the command you call your homebot by, such as {example} {where}: string, where in the file: '<file>:<line>:<col>'; {name_key}: string, the name config key (fixed), name; {example}: string, an example command name (fixed), zork
config.error.name_syntax [error]: {where}: {name_key} "{value}" must be {rule} {where}: string, where in the file: '<file>:<line>:<col>'; {value}: string, the name as written; {name_key}: string, the name config key (fixed), name; {rule}: string, the command name syntax (fixed), 2 to 32 lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, starting with a letter
name.status.configured [status]: Configured name: {name} {name}: string, name in homebot.yml
name.status.effective [status]: Effective name here: {name} {name}: string, command name available on this machine
name.why.link_broken [status]: The recorded link at {path} is missing or points elsewhere. {path}: path, the recorded name link
name.why.not_on_path [status]: The name's link is not on this process's PATH.
name.why.shadowed [status]: Another program at {path} comes before the name's link on PATH. {path}: path, the program that shadows the name
name.why.bad_record [status]: The machine's name record at {path} cannot be read. {path}: path, the machine name record
name.why.no_record [status]: This machine has not recorded that name yet.
name.why.config_off [status]: The machine still has a name link, but homebot.yml has no name.
name.why.recorded_other [status]: This machine still uses {recorded}; run homebot name {configured} to finish the change. {recorded}: string, the working name in this machine's record; {configured}: string, the different name in homebot.yml
name.status.set [success]: Name set to {name}. {name}: string, the new command name
name.status.off [success]: Name turned off; homebot works as before.
name.status.old_stops [warning]: The old name {name} stops working; no forwarding link is left. {name}: string, the old command name
name.warning.in_work_tree [warning]: Your ~/.local/bin is inside a git work tree; another tool could commit this link.
name.hint.path [hint]: Add ~/.local/bin to your PATH to type {name}; homebot works now. {name}: string, the new command name
name.error.refused [error]: Cannot use {name}: {reason} ({path}) {name}: string, the proposed name; {reason}: string, why the name is refused; {path}: path, the blocking path, if any
name.error.refused_no_path [error]: Cannot use {name}: {reason} {name}: string, the proposed name; {reason}: string, why the name is refused
name.reason.syntax [error]: use 2 to 32 lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, starting with a letter
name.reason.reserved [error]: homebot uses it as a command
name.reason.shell [error]: the shell uses that word
name.reason.tool [error]: homebot runs a program by that name
name.reason.on_path [error]: a program with that name is on PATH
name.reason.in_repo [error]: the home repo already places that name in ~/.local/bin
name.reason.foreign [error]: a foreign file already has that name; it was left untouched
name.reason.synced_bin [error]: another tool manages ~/.local/bin
name.reason.temp_blocked [error]: the temporary link is occupied; your current link was kept
name.reason.no_homebot [error]: there is no stable homebot binary to link to
tidy.did.named [status]: kept this machine's name link in sync
tidy.did.name_off [status]: removed this machine's name link
inbox.name_blocked.title [inbox]: The name {name} is blocked on this machine {name}: string, the configured name
inbox.name_shadowed_ours.title [inbox]: The name {name} shadows another program {name}: string, the working name
inbox.name_shadowed_other.title [inbox]: Another program shadows the name {name} {name}: string, the configured name
inbox.name_shadowed.detail [inbox]: The other program is at {path}. {path}: path, the program with the same command name
inbox.name_no_bin.title [inbox]: The name {name} needs a bin directory here {name}: string, the configured name
inbox.name_helper_path.title [inbox]: The helper cannot use the name {name} yet {name}: string, the configured name
setup.error.name_syntax [error]: {flag} {name} must be {rule} {name}: string, the invalid --name value; {flag}: string, the name option (fixed), --name; {rule}: string, the valid command name syntax (fixed), 2 to 32 lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, starting with a letter
setup.error.persona_syntax [error]: {flag} {persona} must {rule} {persona}: string, the invalid --persona value; {flag}: string, the persona option (fixed), --persona; {rule}: string, the valid persona name syntax (fixed), start with a lowercase letter and contain only lowercase letters, digits and hyphens (at most 32 characters)
setup.error.remove_flags [error]: {remove_flag} cannot be combined with {name_flag} or {persona_flag} {remove_flag}: string, the remove option (fixed), --remove; {name_flag}: string, the name option (fixed), --name; {persona_flag}: string, the persona option (fixed), --persona
setup.status.persona [status]: Persona in this repo: {persona} {persona}: string, the selected persona id
setup.status.repo_name [status]: This repo calls your homebot {name}. {name}: string, the name stored in homebot.yml
setup.status.repo_name_wins [status]: This repo calls it {repo}; {name_flag} {flag} was ignored without a terminal. {repo}: string, the name stored in homebot.yml; {flag}: string, the differing --name value; {name_flag}: string, the name option (fixed), --name
setup.prompt.change_name [prompt]: This repo calls it {repo} on every machine. Change the shared name to {flag}? {choice} {repo}: string, the name stored in homebot.yml; {flag}: string, the differing --name value; {choice}: string, the yes or no prompt keys (fixed), [y/N]
setup.prompt.name [prompt]: Give your homebot a command name? For example {example} ({enter} keeps homebot): {example}: string, an example command name (fixed), zork; {enter}: string, the Enter key (fixed), Enter
setup.warning.name_invalid [warning]: {name} must be {rule}. Try another name. {name}: string, the invalid answer to the setup name question; {rule}: string, the valid command name syntax (fixed), 2 to 32 lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, starting with a letter
setup.warning.name_blocked [warning]: {name} cannot be used as a command here: {reason}. Homebot will keep working. {name}: string, the blocked name; {reason}: string, the reason from the machine's name check
setup.prompt.name_path [prompt]: Put {bin} on your {path_env} in {file} so the name works? {choice} {file}: path, the startup file selected for this shell; {bin}: string, the user bin directory (fixed), ~/.local/bin; {path_env}: string, the PATH environment variable (fixed), PATH; {choice}: string, the yes or no prompt keys (fixed), [Y/n]
setup.hint.name_path [hint]: Add {bin} to your {path_env} in {file} to type {name}; homebot works now. {file}: path, the startup file selected for this shell; {name}: string, the configured name; {bin}: string, the user bin directory (fixed), ~/.local/bin; {path_env}: string, the PATH environment variable (fixed), PATH
setup.warning.path_link [warning]: Cannot safely edit {file} (link target: {target}). Add {bin} to your {path_env} yourself. {file}: path, the startup file selected for this shell; {target}: path, where its symlink resolves; {bin}: string, the user bin directory (fixed), ~/.local/bin; {path_env}: string, the PATH environment variable (fixed), PATH
setup.status.path_added [status]: Added {bin} to {file}. {file}: path, the startup file that received the PATH line; {bin}: string, the user bin directory (fixed), ~/.local/bin
setup.status.changed_here [status]: Changed on this machine:
setup.status.name_link [status]:   name link: {bin}{name} points to {binary} {name}: string, the configured name; {bin}: string, the user bin directory with separator (fixed), ~/.local/bin/; {binary}: string, the stable binary name (fixed), homebot
setup.status.path_line [status]:   added the {path_env} startup line {path_env}: string, the PATH environment variable (fixed), PATH
setup.status.name_blocked [status]:   no name link for {name} was made here {name}: string, the shared name blocked on this machine
setup.status.name_uncommitted [status]: The new name is in {config} but stays uncommitted with your other work; your next save carries it. {config}: string, the config file (fixed), homebot.yml
